lesson k-travel-long-ago v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=5 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "How people travelled long ago"
  goal "Say how people travelled long ago and how they travel now, and put ways of travelling in order from oldest to newest."

  look {
    scene 32x18 {
      thenandnow street year=1850 sign=0 things=["horse and cart", "hot-air balloon"] at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      Long, long ago there were no cars, trains or aeroplanes. People walked, rode horses, and rode in
      carts pulled by horses; they crossed the sea in ships with sails. Cars came later, and later
      still the first aeroplanes flew.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.older-way k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.older-way count=2 seed=2
    practice k.how-they-went count=3 seed=3 easy-count=2 hard-count=2
    show k.travel-street
    practice k.long-ago-or-now count=2 seed=8 easy-count=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.why-horses
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.travel-in-order
  }
  remember {
    say "Long ago: horses, carts and sailing ships. Now: cars, trains and aeroplanes."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. Old pictures and a trip to a transport museum make this real, and so
    does a question to an older relative: how did you get to school? The idea to watch is that long
    ago means a few years; a horse and cart is from before great-great-grandparents. If the child is
    stuck, sort toy vehicles into long ago and now. England, Reception, ELG Past and Present
    (recalled, not fetched): "Know some similarities and differences between things in the past and
    now, drawing on their experiences and what has been read in class".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a horse and cart is worked in the teacher's pen first, the pairs to judge are horse and car, and sail and aeroplane, and the ways to go are long ago."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the pairs are a bicycle and an electric car, and a steam train and a jet, and the ways to go are today's."
  }
}
